---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: traefik
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
  annotations:
    # The annotation, not spec.loadBalancerIP — that field is deprecated upstream and MetalLB
    # reads this. This is the only way to claim an address out of the autoAssign: false
    # ingress-pool (roles/setup/k3s/templates/metallb-pool.yaml.j2).
    #
    # metallb.io/, NOT metallb.universe.tf/. The prefix flipped: MetalLB v0.15 introduced
    # metallb.io/ for Service annotations and deprecated metallb.universe.tf/, and this
    # cluster runs v0.16.0 (k3s_metallb_version). Both prefixes are still read
    # (controller/service.go valueForAnnotation, metallb.io wins), but the deprecated one
    # emits a `deprecatedAnnotation` Warning Event per Service on every reconcile.
    #
    # Kubernetes accepts any annotation key, and MetalLB ignores ones it does not recognise,
    # so a wrong prefix is silent: the Service is created, an address is assigned from the
    # auto-assign pool instead of the reserved VIP, and nothing anywhere reports a problem.
    # That is why the assert in tasks/main.yml checks the landed address rather than
    # trusting the annotation. Every pinned-address Service here has that assert.
    metallb.io/loadBalancerIPs: {{ k3s_metallb_ingress_vip }}
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  # Local, NOT the Cluster default, and this is a security control rather than a tuning knob.
  # Under Cluster the node masquerades inbound traffic, so Traefik sees every client as
  # cni0's 10.42.0.1 — which is inside 10.0.0.0/8, so Authelia's access_control rules would
  # score ALL traffic, including public traffic after slice 6, as RFC1918 LAN and hand it the
  # one_factor policy meant for the local network. Local preserves the real client address.
  # The scheduling question this used to defer to "slice 7" was answered when daniel-server
  # joined on 2026-08-14, and the answer is the `# DECIDED:` marker on the nodeSelector in
  # deployment.yaml.j2: announcement and placement move as a unit, permanently. ETP Local is
  # what makes that binding — a VIP-backed pod on the non-announcing node makes the announcer
  # drop all forwarded LAN traffic.
  externalTrafficPolicy: Local
  # The node's OWN address as a second front door (slice-6 B5, 2026-08-09). The router's
  # port-forward UI only targets devices from its DHCP/ARP list, and the MetalLB VIP has no
  # lease and never will — so the 80/443 forward points at the DEVICE daniel-box, and
  # kube-proxy answers on the node IP for this Service. The VIP stays the LAN-facing name
  # (Pi-hole overrides, wg-easy, bridge transports all keep using .240); this field exists
  # for the WAN path only. Local policy applies to externalIPs the same way, so client
  # addresses stay real. The forward follows whichever node this IP belongs to, which is why
  # it renders from k8s_primary_node rather than a literal — settled by the same 2026-08-14
  # decision as the policy above.
  externalIPs:
    - {{ hostvars[k8s_primary_node].server_ip }}
  selector:
    app: traefik
  ports:
    - name: http
      port: 80
      targetPort: http
    - name: https
      port: 443
      targetPort: https
